# The alignment canary, re-exposed rather than recomputed.
#
# `Ring::on_distinct_lines` delegates to `CursorPair::on_distinct_lines`, which
# is unconditionally true by the reasoning in `ring_cursor.accepted`. A wrapper
# around a constant is a constant. Re-exposed here so a caller holding a `Ring`
# can make the check without reaching for the pair.
#
# See accepted/readme.md, "The alignment canaries".

ring_spsc/src/lib.rs:431:5: replace Ring<S>::on_distinct_lines -> bool with true

# The bounded drain's reload test, off by one in the harmless direction.
#
# `Consumer::drain_up_to` reloads the producer cursor only when the records it
# already knows of are fewer than `max`. Turning `<` into `<=` also reloads when
# they are exactly `max`. The batch is `max` long either way, because the
# producer cursor only advances, so no input separates the two by what the call
# returns or what the ring does next. The mutant costs one load of the line the
# producer writes. That load is what the cache exists to skip, and only a load
# counter or a clock sees it.
#
# The same kind as `ring_bench`'s timing-only entries: killable in principle,
# by instrumenting `ring_spsc`'s cursors with a counting cell, which this crate
# does not do. The behavioural half (`<` against `==` and `>`) is defended:
# both of those time out against
# `a_bounded_drain_returns_everything_published_up_to_its_bound` and the
# threaded tests.

ring_spsc/src/lib.rs:1048:60: replace < with <= in Consumer<'_, S>::drain_up_to
